{"review":{"at":"2026-09-28T13:29:22-05:00","size":"full","verdict":"fix","findings":[{"n":1,"kind":"fix","text":"The same class names are styled differently by different cards, and none of those rules are scoped. `.figma-panel`, `.figma-scale`, `.figma-chip` and `.figma-compare` are defined bare in 5-2-col-sidebar-content/view.css:8-15, 6-ui-controls/view.css:8-18,71,143 and 7-task-management-ai-patterns/view.css:24-60, and `.figma-badge` in both 6/view.css:~120 and 7/view.css:99. Every stylesheet stays loaded once the AI 2 board shows all seven cards on one page, so whichever loads last wins. Card 6's `.figma-panel { display:flex; gap:3.4em; font-size:clamp(…cqi…) }` then lands on the Typography, Lists, Sidebar and Task panels too, and card 6's pill `.figma-chip` replaces card 7's box chip. Each card was shot on its own preview page, so this collision never showed up in testing. Fix: scope every rule under that card's own root class, as 1-typography/view.css and 2-lists/view.css already do (`.figma-typography .figma-panel`), or give each card its own prefix."},{"n":2,"kind":"note","text":"Deliverable 3 said to reuse ui/ux components first, but the only one imported is `ui/avatar`. Card 6 (6-ui-controls/view.js) is literally a \"UI Controls\" gallery, yet it hand-builds its toggle, chips, progress bars and count badges instead of using ui/controls, ui/tags, ui/progress and ui/badge. Nothing new was put in ui/ or ux/. If those parts could not match the Figma, the landing report should say why for each one."},{"n":3,"kind":"note","text":"Deliverables 1 and 4 cannot be checked from this diff. It has no group card \"Figma: Sept 2026\", no page.jsonl placements (Servex writes those), and no 1920 proof shots beside the Figma pngs with a per-section list of what matches and what doesn't. Before landing, check the seven cards exist under the group card, and link the shots and the lists in the outcome."},{"n":4,"kind":"note","text":"Once item 1 is fixed, open the whole group on the board at 1920 with all seven cards visible together and shoot it. That is the only view where cross-card leaks show up."},{"n":5,"kind":"note","text":"4-2-col-pricing/view.js:53 adds the \"chosen\" status line to the end of the view, below the Figma screenshot. It does not appear under the plan that was clicked, as its comment claims. Put it inside `.figma-pricing-scale`, or under the clicked plan."},{"n":6,"kind":"note","text":"7-task-management-ai-patterns/ai.js:~250: `pick()` uses `.click(function(){ this.ac(\"chosen\") })`. This only works if the click binding sets `this` to the button View rather than to the DOM element. Check it once in the browser, or capture the returned button and call `.ac()` on that instead."},{"n":7,"kind":"note","text":"Its comment says 2-lists/view.js:8 uses an \"auto-fit grid\", but view.css:12-20 switched to a fixed `1fr 1fr`. The comment is out of date."},{"n":8,"kind":"note","text":"Simpler: cards 5, 6 and 7 each repeat the same \"scale, don't reflow\" block (`container-type: inline-size` + `font-size: clamp(7px, calc(100cqi / 53.5), 16px)`) and the same Figma-compare block. One small shared piece (for example `ux/figma-frame`: a scaled panel plus the comparison image) would remove that repetition. It would also satisfy the \"reusable goes in ui/ux\" rule, and it would end the collisions in item 1."}],"branch":"worktree/qf-2","head":"4902ac3840851de999f83ee43055c36de438dde6","model":"claude-opus-5-5","cost":0.7713826,"file":"review.md"}}
